experiment: sensefit
  instrument        sensefit   trial seed 00000000013578de   6 creatures
                    another probe is another trial sequence: its numbers
                    are not comparable with these (~0.05 at 300 trials)
  the question      what does the creature's OWN error signal look
                    like? aimsense needs a per-tick sigma and a
                    correlation time; selfcode measured neither.
  why it matters    matched on selfcode's TRIAL accuracy, a white and
                    a correlated sensor remove 10.5% and 37.6% of the
                    open-loop error. The difference is the number
                    below, so it is measured and not argued.

  seed   r(held out)  shuffled   FIXED-F1 r   resid sd   resid tau   F1 sd   F1 tau   trial acc
  0         +0.408    -0.015       +0.064      22.1     293.2    24.2   342.2     0.724
  1         +0.298    -0.002       +0.025      20.9     282.5    21.7   334.3     0.707
  2         +0.388    +0.007       +0.128      19.1     251.7    20.4   330.6     0.759
  3         +0.362    +0.000       -0.008      21.1     302.2    22.6   336.8     0.828
  4         +0.399    +0.002       +0.009      19.7     287.7    21.5   341.3     0.759
  5         +0.384    +0.017       -0.041      22.0     284.3    23.8   349.3     0.828

  r held out        +0.373 +/- 0.016
  shuffled control  +0.001 +/- 0.004   (must be ~0)
  FIXED-F1 control  +0.029 +/- 0.024   (selfcode's matched control)
  excess over fixed +0.344 +/- 0.029

  THE RAW RESIDUAL, which is NOT the sensor's noise
    pred - F1 sigma           20.8 +/- 0.5 Hz
    its correlation tau       283.6 +/- 7.0 ms
    F1's own sd / tau         22.4 Hz / 339.1 ms
    -> the raw residual is 93% of the signal's size and 84% of its
       correlation time, because what an attenuated decoder misses IS
       F1-shaped. Decompose before calling any of it noise.

  THE DECOMPOSITION: observed = b + alpha*F1 + eta
    alpha (attenuation)       0.1581 +/- 0.0134   -> a controller undoes
                                                 this with gain x6.3
    eta sigma (independent)   8.7 +/- 0.5 Hz
    eta correlation tau       61.8 +/- 4.8 ms
    INPUT-REFERRED sigma      56.2 +/- 3.1 Hz   <- what aimsense needs
    self-check  sd(F1)*sqrt(1-r^2)/r = 55.6 Hz against the measured 56.2
    against F1's own sd       22.4 Hz  -> signal-to-noise 0.40
    trial-level accuracy      0.767 +/- 0.021   (selfcode: 0.623)

  GUARD  shuffled collapses?            OK
  GUARD  beats the FIXED-F1 control?    OK
  GUARD  eta faster than F1 itself?     OK, eta is genuinely noise

  HAND THESE TO aimsense: input-referred sigma 56 Hz at tau 62 ms,
  with the controller gain raised x6.3 to undo the attenuation. The raw
  residual (21 Hz) is the WRONG number to hand over and it is the one
  this experiment was built to compute before the decomposition existed.
  Trial accuracy 0.767 against selfcode's 0.623 is the comparability
  check: a CONTINUOUS regression and a binary median split on binned
  spike counts are different decoders, so they need not agree exactly,
  but both must be well clear of 0.500 and both are.
